<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 1st Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.3)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Shapes on Our Plates</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> It's a Round Orange.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Text: Use pictures or objects to express ideas. Concept: Use facial expressions and simple gestures to help explain feelings or needs.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can use gestures or body language to describe food items (e.g., pretending to hold a round apple). Can clarify responses using pictures or pointing to food-related visuals (e.g., showing the picture of a cracker to clarify its meaning). Can use facial expressions and gestures to help describe food preferences and shapes (e.g., pretending to take a bite of an apple and using the hands to show that it is round).</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center; margin-top: 20px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 34%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher leads a "Shape Walk" around the classroom, pointing to objects and asking "What shape is it?" (e.g., "What shape is the clock? It's round!"). Students respond chorally or individually. Teacher then shows flashcards of target food items (orange, cracker, banana, lemon) and asks students to recall their names and shapes, using simple gestures (e.g., drawing a circle for "round").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a flashcard of an orange and says, "This is a round orange." The teacher makes a large circular gesture with both hands to show "round," emphasizing the /r/ sound. Then the teacher holds up a cracker flashcard and says, "This is a square cracker," making a square shape with hands and emphasizing the /k/ sound. The teacher also models facial expressions for "tasty!" (smile) and "no, thank you" (gentle head shake).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Holding a round apple flashcard) "Is this a *square* apple?" (Students shake heads or say "No!"). "What shape is it?" (Students say "Round!").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Making a round gesture) "Show me *round* with your hands." (Students mimic the gesture). "Show me *square*." (Students mimic the gesture).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Shapes on Our Plates".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Large picture cards or realia of various food items (orange, pineapple, lemon, lime, mango, papaya, cracker, banana, juice box), pre-drawn shape outlines (round, square, oval) on a large poster titled "Shapes on Our Plates."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Identifying food shapes: Identifying and describing foods by shape (e.g., "The cracker is square."). Describing food: Using simple words to describe the colors and shapes of fruits and other foods, such as "orange" and "cracker."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Activities & Teacher Actions:</b> The teacher displays the "Shapes on Our Plates" poster. The teacher presents each food item (or picture) one by one, asking "What is this?" and "What shape is it?" The teacher explicitly uses gestures to help students understand the shapes. For instance, holding a banana and making an oval shape with hands, saying, "This is a banana. It's oval." Then, the teacher places the food picture under the correct shape outline on the poster. This includes all target vocabulary (pineapple, lemon, lime, mango, papaya, cracker, banana, juice box) and shapes (round, square, oval).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Point to the round fruit!" (Students point to orange, lemon, lime).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Show me the cracker!" (Students point to the cracker). "What shape is the cracker?" (Students say "Square!").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Mediation.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice mediating information about food shapes using gestures, pointing, and simple descriptive sentences. Grammar: Adjective + noun ("round orange"), WH-questions ("What shape is it?"), Present simple ("Oranges are round."). Vocabulary: All target words (pineapple, lemon, lime, mango, papaya, cracker, banana, food, juice box, snack, meal, eat, cut, hold, see, mix, round, square, oval, big, small, healthy, tasty, orange, black, brown, white, this, that). Pronunciation: /r/ in "round" and /k/ in "cracker", /æ/ in "apple".</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Guided Gestures & Description (Controlled Practice):</b> The teacher holds up a flashcard of a food item (e.g., a lemon). The teacher says, "This is a lemon. It's round." while making a round gesture. Students repeat the sentence and gesture. The teacher asks, "What shape is it?" Students respond, "It's round!" with the gesture. This is repeated for various foods, focusing on /r/ in "round" and /k/ in "cracker."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. "Guess My Food Shape" (Semi-controlled Mediation):</b> The teacher prepares a "mystery bag" with small toy foods or cutouts. A student pulls out a food item (e.g., banana) but keeps it hidden from the class. The student then uses gestures, body language, and simple descriptive words (e.g., "It's long," *stretches hands out*; "It's yellow," *points to something yellow*; "It's oval," *draws oval in air*) to help classmates guess the food and its shape. Classmates ask, "Is it a banana?" The student can nod or shake head, or give more gestures. The teacher helps scaffold language: "Say, 'It's a long, yellow banana.'" This explicitly targets using facial expressions and simple gestures to explain.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. "Explain the Plate" (Pair Mediation & Peer Check):</b> Students work in pairs with a small collection of food flashcards and shape cutouts (round, square, oval). One student picks a food flashcard (e.g., cracker) and *points to it* and *gestures its shape* without saying the shape name. They might say, "This is a cracker." and make a square shape with their hands. The other student must then find the correct shape cutout (square) and say, "It's a square cracker!" or "The cracker is square!" They then switch roles. This activity encourages clarifying responses using pictures or pointing and using gestures to describe.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Shapes on Our Plates."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Small group presentations of the "Food Shape Sorting" project. Students will present their pre-prepared sorting boards (e.g., poster boards with food pictures/cutouts sorted into "Round Food," "Square Food," "Oval Food" sections).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Activities & Teacher Actions:</b> Students present their "Food Shape Sorting" projects to their small groups (3-4 students) or to the teacher. Each student takes a turn to point to and explain at least two food items on their board. They must use gestures and simple sentences to describe the food and its shape. For example, a student points to an orange on their board and says, "This is a round orange," while making a circular gesture with their hands. Then, they might point to a cracker and say, "This is a square cracker," making a square gesture. If a group member seems confused, the presenter must use more gestures or point more clearly to the pictures to clarify. The teacher circulates, offering support and prompting for gestures/clarification: "Can you show us 'round' with your hands?" "Is that a *big* orange or a *small* orange?" (eliciting adjective + noun and vocabulary).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This activity directly integrates the 21st Century Skills Project by having students mediate information about their sorted foods, clearly demonstrating their understanding and explanation skills.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the small group presentations.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each student presents their "Food Shape Sorting" project to the teacher or a small group, identifying at least two food items and their shapes. They must use gestures or pointing to help describe and clarify their explanations. (e.g., "This is a pineapple. It's big!" *opens hands wide*. "This is a cracker. It's square!" *makes square gesture*).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can use gestures/body language to describe food items and their shapes (Mediation: Learning Outcome).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can clarify responses using pictures or pointing to food-related visuals (Mediation: Learning Outcome).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses "Adjective + noun" structures (e.g., "round orange," "square cracker").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses target vocabulary (e.g., orange, cracker, round, square, big, small).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Pronunciation of /r/ in "round" and /k/ in "cracker" is intelligible.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students draw their favorite food and its shape on a small paper. Below the drawing, they complete these sentences or draw a happy/sad face:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can show <u>round</u> with my hands." (Yes / No)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can tell my friend: 'This is a ____ ____'." (They write the food and shape words).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">7-8 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What did you like about showing your shapes and foods to your friends?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was hard to explain using your hands or pointing?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What specific gestures and pointing techniques did students use most effectively to mediate information about food shapes?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Were students able to clarify meaning using visuals when needed? What adjustments or additional scaffolds (e.g., more direct modeling of specific gestures) are needed for future mediation tasks?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How well did the "Food Shape Sorting" project integrate the linguistic and mediation objectives?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson concluded the "Shapes on Our Plates" theme. The next lesson will introduce a new theme and set of vocabulary, potentially incorporating simple mediation tasks from the beginning to reinforce the skill of using non-verbal cues and visuals to understand and explain.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>